media: v4l2-core: a read-only ioctl keeps its ALWAYS_COPY flag

0136m gave a source the display firmware's mode table does not carry its own
answer, -ERANGE with the measured timing, and 0136v filled that timing in at
the INCAP sanity check, where such a source really ends. Both wrote the
measurement into the ioctl argument on the strength of VIDIOC_QUERY_DV_TIMINGS
carrying INFO_FL_ALWAYS_COPY, which is what makes v4l2 hand results back
although the call failed. The device said otherwise: dev21, 22.09.2026, the
kernel printed "source 1600x900 at 60.00 Hz: in none of the firmware's mode
records -- no lock" while h713-tv printed 0x0p?, 0.0 MHz for the same source.

The flag never reached the decision. video_get_user() takes the read-only
shortcut

	if (!(_IOC_DIR(cmd) & _IOC_WRITE)) {
		memset(parg, 0, n);
		return 0;
	}

before it looks the ioctl's flags up, so *always_copy is still false when
video_usercopy() asks

	if (err < 0 && !always_copy)
		goto out;

and the filled struct is thrown away. VIDIOC_QUERY_DV_TIMINGS is _IOR('V', 99,
struct v4l2_dv_timings) - read-only - and it is the only entry in v4l2_ioctls[]
that is both read-only and ALWAYS_COPY (G_EDID, S_EDID and the three EXT_CTRLS
are _IOWR and take the long path), so for that one ioctl the flag has had no
effect at all.

The lookup moves in front of the shortcut. Nothing else changes: the flags word
is read exactly where it was used, INFO_FL_CLEAR_MASK still only narrows the
copy-in of a writing ioctl, and for a read-only ioctl without the flag
always_copy stays false, as before. The blast radius is one ioctl on failure,
where the caller gets the zeroed or driver-filled struct v4l2_ioctls already
promises it.

What the HY310 sees: on a locked source QUERY_DV_TIMINGS returns 0 and this
code path is not reached - err < 0 is the only case the flag decides. What
changes is the refused source: h713-tv's `ctl status` and its journal line name
the raster and the pixel clock instead of 0x0p?, 0.0 MHz.

Follow-up to 0136m/0136v (Q16, from the dev21 run of 22.09.2026). Not a
h713 file: this is v4l2 core, and the fix is upstream material - the flag is
documented behaviour that one direction of ioctl silently loses.

Tested on the HY310: not yet. The device proof is a 1600x900 source on the
debug kernel: `h713-tv ctl status` must show
"signal          1600x900p60, 108.0 MHz not in the firmware's table -- console".

diff -ruN a/drivers/media/v4l2-core/v4l2-ioctl.c b/drivers/media/v4l2-core/v4l2-ioctl.c
--- a/drivers/media/v4l2-core/v4l2-ioctl.c
+++ b/drivers/media/v4l2-core/v4l2-ioctl.c
@@ -3271,8 +3271,20 @@
 			  bool *always_copy)
 {
 	unsigned int n = _IOC_SIZE(real_cmd);
+	u32 flags = 0;
 	int err = 0;
 
+	/*
+	 * INFO_FL_ALWAYS_COPY is read below the read-only shortcut, so it has
+	 * to be taken before it: a _IOR ioctl that carries the flag returns
+	 * results the caller needs even when it fails, and dropping them is
+	 * the one thing the flag exists to prevent.
+	 */
+	if (v4l2_is_known_ioctl(real_cmd)) {
+		flags = v4l2_ioctls[_IOC_NR(real_cmd)].flags;
+		*always_copy = flags & INFO_FL_ALWAYS_COPY;
+	}
+
 	if (!(_IOC_DIR(cmd) & _IOC_WRITE)) {
 		/* read-only ioctl */
 		memset(parg, 0, n);
@@ -3286,13 +3298,8 @@
 	 * with that index.  We only need to copy up the first
 	 * non-input field.
 	 */
-	if (v4l2_is_known_ioctl(real_cmd)) {
-		u32 flags = v4l2_ioctls[_IOC_NR(real_cmd)].flags;
-
-		if (flags & INFO_FL_CLEAR_MASK)
-			n = (flags & INFO_FL_CLEAR_MASK) >> 16;
-		*always_copy = flags & INFO_FL_ALWAYS_COPY;
-	}
+	if (flags & INFO_FL_CLEAR_MASK)
+		n = (flags & INFO_FL_CLEAR_MASK) >> 16;
 
 	if (cmd == real_cmd) {
 		if (copy_from_user(parg, (void __user *)arg, n))
